    "prompt": "[Reference] @image1 is the female lead’s character sheet. Use her face, age, hair, and body proportions. All views depict one woman; do not duplicate the character. Do not reproduce the sheet’s background, poses, labels, or layout.\n\n[Concept and Visual Treatment]\nA realistic fashion mini-comedy: a woman hails a taxi outside a Parisian hotel, a giant white rabbit picks her up and jumps over a car. She treats this extraordinary ride as an ordinary comfortable service.\n\nEarly autumn in Paris: luxurious hotel doors, brass details, cream-colored stone, wrought-iron balconies, a few golden leaves on dry pavement. Warm sunlight from the side and behind, soft natural light on her face. The visual character of footage shot on an ARRI Alexa Mini LF with ARRI Signature Prime lenses. Natural skin, detailed white fur without clipped highlights, contact shadows, natural motion blur. Vertical composition, keeping her face and key actions inside the frame.\n\nOne white rabbit the size of a small car: realistic anatomy, dark eyes, a pink nose, long mobile ears. A compact dusty-rose velvet saddle is strapped securely behind its shoulders, with a front grab handle and short stirrups. Fur compresses beneath the straps. Keep the rabbit’s size and saddle construction unchanged.\n\n[Shot Sequence]\n0–2 s.\nMedium shot framed to mid-thigh, 50 mm lens. The eye-level camera tracks gently backward in front of the woman. She exits the hotel doorway, looks along the street, and raises her hand to shoulder height to hail a taxi. Three-quarter face view, focus on her eyes. The shadow of enormous rabbit ears glides across the door behind her shoulder. Hard cut.\n\n2–3 s.\nTight detail, 85 mm lens, stationary camera at pavement level. An enormous white front paw settles beside the woman’s foot. Fur compresses under the animal’s weight. Hard cut.\n\n3–5 s.\nFull shot from a close distance, 35 mm lens, stationary side camera. The woman and the entire rabbit fill most of the frame, with its ears uncropped. She lowers her hand and looks at it calmly with a barely perceptible smile. The rabbit crouches beside her, lowering the saddle, and holds still for mounting.\n\n5–7 s.\nMedium side three-quarter shot, 50 mm lens. The woman grips the saddle handle, places one foot in the near stirrup, and lifts herself up. The camera rises slightly with her. Cut on the upward movement; omit the completion of mounting across the edit. The next shot begins with her already securely seated.\n\n7–9 s.\nSide tracking shot, 35 mm lens. The camera travels parallel, keeping the woman in focus. The rabbit races left to right in long, low bounds. The woman holds the handle, feet in the stirrups. A small stationary car is clearly established ahead, positioned across the narrow passage; beyond it is empty pavement. The rabbit approaches the car.\n\n9–12 s.\nWide side shot, 28 mm lens. The camera stays on the same side of the action axis and smoothly follows the movement to the right. One uninterrupted shot: the rabbit gathers its hind legs, pushes off, jumps over the car’s roof, and lands beyond it. Keep the rabbit with rider, car, and landing area visible simultaneously. A clear ballistic arc and visible roof clearance, without hovering. The front paws touch down first, followed by the hind paws; the joints bend to absorb the weight. The woman leans slightly forward and rises in the stirrups while holding the handle. The ears bounce naturally after landing. Normal speed, no contact with the car.\n\n12–15 s.\nClose-up of the woman from the front in three-quarter view, 50 mm lens. The camera matches her speed, keeping her face in focus while sunlit facades blur behind her. The rabbit continues in gentler bounds. One ear tilts backward, briefly obscuring part of her face. Keeping one hand on the saddle handle, she calmly moves the ear aside with the other. Relaxed shoulders, a small satisfied smile. End on her unobstructed face.\n\n[Music and Sound]\nOne continuous instrumental track: light French jazz with a contemporary groove, piano, upright bass, brushed drums, and pizzicato strings. No vocals or stereotypical accordion.\n\n0–2 s: restrained piano and bass; footsteps and street ambience remain audible.\n2–3 s: a brief musical pause on paw contact; a soft heavy footfall and a short wordless notification chime.\n3–7 s: the main theme returns, joined by playful pizzicato strings and brushes.\n7–9 s: rhythmic density increases without changing tempo; a rising phrase reinforces acceleration, with audible wind and paw impacts.\n9–12 s: a musical accent on takeoff; percussion drops out during flight, leaving a sustained string chord and rushing air; the full groove returns on landing.\n12–15 s: the light main theme, a quiet piano note on the ear gesture, and a resolving chord on her smile. Wind and rustling fur remain audible.\n\n[Continuity]\nOne woman, one rabbit, one saddle, one car. Preserve the woman’s face and proportions. Maintain left-to-right travel, consistent shadows, realistic weight, and stable contact with the saddle. Only the animal’s scale is absurd; everything else looks live-action. No cartoon movement, toy-like fur, distortions, extra limbs, objects passing through each other, or people in the jump path. No dialogue, voice-over, laughter, generated text, subtitles, or logos."
