    "prompt": "Create an 8-second, 16:9 photorealistic cinematic sequence. The central effect is a precisely composed match cut between two butterfly wing markings and a woman’s irises, concealed by darkness. It is NOT a butterfly flying into the lens and NOT a visible transformation.\n\nREFERENCES\n@image1: butterfly appearance and its two dominant iris-like wing markings.\n@image2: the dark red Chinese imperial palace corridor.\n@image3: the woman’s identity, hairstyle, makeup, jewelry, and costume. Render one woman, not a character sheet.\n\nAttached video: reference for the transition’s visual continuity only. The explicit framing and action instructions below take priority.\n\nPLAN THE MATCH BEFORE THE ACTION\nFirst establish the woman’s final head-and-shoulders portrait composition. Her shoulders are turned approximately 30 degrees toward screen right. Her face is turned only slightly in that direction, with both eyes clearly visible, and her gaze returns toward the lens. Her chin is subtly raised. A delicate head tilt places her screen-right iris slightly higher than her screen-left iris.\n\nNow compose the butterfly’s final wing presentation to match that portrait: the centers of its two dominant wing markings must already coincide with the woman’s future iris centers. Match their spacing, apparent diameter, diagonal alignment, and shallow perspective BEFORE the blackout begins.\n\nThe butterfly must remain small enough to fit fully inside the image, approximately one third of the frame width, with ample corridor visible on every side. Adjust the portrait framing and butterfly presentation coherently in advance. Never solve the match by bringing the butterfly closer to the lens, moving the markings through darkness, or distorting the woman’s anatomy.\n\n0.0–3.0 SECONDS: APPROACH AND ALIGNMENT\nA stationary camera looks down the palace corridor. The butterfly approaches from the distance, initially out of focus. During the second second, focus shifts onto it.\n\nIts flight has small irregular rises, settling dips, sideways deviations, and corrective banking, naturally connected to its wingbeats. It must look like a lightweight living insect, not a rigid object sliding toward the viewer.\n\nThe forward approach slows and ends at a moderate distance from the camera. The butterfly never exceeds approximately 35% of the frame width.\n\nNear 3.0 seconds, it makes a brief braking wingbeat and a subtle bank, presenting both patterned wing surfaces clearly at a shallow oblique angle. Its two markings reach the preplanned positions of the woman’s irises. The slight diagonal and perspective correspond to her head angle.\n\nTrigger the blackout at this aligned instant. Do not continue flying toward the camera. Do not hold an unnaturally frozen butterfly in an illuminated shot.\n\n3.0–3.2 SECONDS: REMOVE THE SURROUNDINGS\nA short, subdued interruption of the lantern light takes the scene into complete black. The corridor, mist, body, and wing surfaces disappear. Only the two faint iris-like markings remain visible.\n\nThe wings do not cover the lens. There is no enlargement, camera movement, white flash, or bright strobe.\n\n3.2–4.2 SECONDS: HOLD THE EXACT TWO MARKINGS\nThe image is pure black except for the same two dim markings.\n\nTheir positions, visible diameter, center-to-center distance, diagonal alignment, and orientation are completely fixed. Treat this interval as holding the same registered pair of shapes in the image, not watching two objects moving through space.\n\nNo receding, shrinking, drifting, rotation, spreading apart, drawing together, or changing perspective. No face, eyelids, eye whites, wing outlines, or mist is visible.\n\nThe markings have barely perceptible muted copper detail and dark centers. They are not glowing eyeballs. No neon, bright white centers, halos, or pulsing.\n\nConceal the substitution of wing-pattern texture with iris texture at this low visibility. Preserve the exact apparent boundaries and positions of the two shapes.\n\n4.2–6.0 SECONDS: REVEAL THE ALREADY ALIGNED WOMAN\nRestore warm lantern illumination, revealing the woman’s face around those same stationary irises.\n\nShe is ALREADY at her final portrait scale, distance, head angle, and screen position before the light returns. She does not move into alignment. Her face does not approach from the background. The camera does not pull back from her eyes.\n\nThe two markings are now her natural irises, in the same locations and at the same apparent size. Only the surrounding face becomes visible. There is no second pair of eyes appearing over them.\n\nMaintain the planned three-quarter shoulder position, slight facial turn, delicate head tilt, raised chin, and direct gaze. She is calm, self-assured, and subtly aloof. Keep her head still during the reveal\n\nUse a clean illumination reveal, not a prolonged overlapping dissolve. No visible wings on her face, anatomical morphing, transparent butterfly, or glowing-eye effect. The butterfly is absent from this portrait.\n\n6.0–8.0 SECONDS: STANDING WOMAN AND FINGER LANDING\nMake one clean cut to a wider stationary shot. The woman is STANDING in the corridor, framed from above her hairstyle to just below her knees. Her knees are visible; her lower legs and feet are cropped out. No sitting, crouching, chair, or full-body framing.\n\nPreserve her three-quarter orientation. Her left forearm is already partly raised at the cut. She completes a small, graceful lifting gesture, offering her relaxed index finger near upper-chest height on screen right\n\nThe same butterfly enters from screen right, already close to her hand and naturally small relative to her fingers. Her eyes follow it, then her head turns smoothly toward it. Her expression remains composed and untroubled.\n\nBy approximately 7.2 seconds, the butterfly brakes, extends its legs, and lands on her index finger. Show clear contact between its feet and her skin. It stays perched through the final frame, gently opening and closing its wings. It does not fly past her hand or take off again.\n\nHer hand settles naturally. She watches the butterfly with quiet confidence. Subtle movement of silk sleeves and jewelry follows her gesture.\n\nLOOK AND CONTINUITY\nRich but desaturated burgundy, crimson, dark wood, bronze, and muted gold. Warm flame-lit palace lanterns and oil lamps only. Real skin texture, delicate wing scales, detailed silk and embroidery, natural motion blur, and restrained cinematic grain\n\nThin white mist drifts softly in illuminated shots and disappears completely during the black interval\n\nThe defining visual rule: position the butterfly correctly BEFORE darkness, hold both markings perfectly unchanged THROUGH darkness, and reveal the woman already aligned AFTER darkness.\n\nNo extra people, duplicate butterflies, extra eyes, malformed hands, text, logos, or watermarks."
